<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch03_P03_Ex6_Exponential_sum</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Conjecture: for all real numbers \(x\), \(\mathrm{e}^{2x}=\mathrm{e}^x+\mathrm{e}^2\).</p>
<p>Disprove it.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Choose a value of \(x\).</p>
<div>\(x=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>At your value of \(x\), work out each side exactly. Type \(\mathrm{e}^2\) as e^2.</p>
<div>\(\mathrm{e}^{2x}=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<div>\(\mathrm{e}^x+\mathrm{e}^2=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A disproof by counterexample chooses a value, works out both sides exactly, and checks that they differ.</p>
<p>Take \(x=0\). Then \(\mathrm{e}^{2x}=\mathrm{e}^0=1\), while \(\mathrm{e}^x+\mathrm{e}^2=1+\mathrm{e}^2\). Since \(\mathrm{e}^2\gt 0\), \(1\neq 1+\mathrm{e}^2\), so the conjecture is false.</p>
<p>The correct law of indices is \(\mathrm{e}^{2x}=\left(\mathrm{e}^x\right)^2\). The conjecture mixes up a sum with a product: \(\mathrm{e}^x\times\mathrm{e}^2=\mathrm{e}^{x+2}\), but \(\mathrm{e}^x+\mathrm{e}^2\) cannot be written as a single power.</p>
<p>The equation does have one solution. Writing \(u=\mathrm{e}^x\), it becomes \(u^2-u-\mathrm{e}^2=0\), which has one positive root, so the equation is true for exactly one value, \(x=\ln\left(\dfrac{1+\sqrt{1+4\mathrm{e}^2}}{2}\right)\approx 1.18\). So a conjecture can be true at a value you happen to try, and every other value of \(x\) is a counterexample.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
